// `__array_new__` filled with an ARRAY LITERAL, into an annotated nested-list
// destination. `vl check` is rc 0 and the module fails to validate: the literal is built
// at the OUTER list's heap types instead of the element's, so `array.new` over the
// backing gets an `i32` where it wants the inner wrapper. Naming the same literal first
// (`const s: i32[] = [0, 0]`) runs, the un-annotated destination runs, an object-literal
// fill runs, and routing the call through a generic `T[]` constructor runs — the fill is
// an identifier there, which is what makes this the literal's own cell rather than
// D1605's. `string[][]` and a struct element are the same cell with a different message.
// Should print 0.
const rows: i32[][] = __array_new__(2, [0, 0])
print(rows[1][0])
